 "title": "Дима ждёт  (Dima is Waiting)",
 "level": "A1 — Duolingo Section 2",
 "labels": {
  "barinov": "Шеф",
  "hostess": "Хостес"
 },
 "segments": [
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Back at the restaurant, Barinov has let the owner's calls ring all morning, and now he walks in. Here it is in Russian.",
   "gap": 0.8
  },
  {
   "v": "hostess",
   "t": "Здравствуйте.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Доброе утро.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "hostess",
   "t": "Дима тебя ждёт.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Дима? Он здесь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "hostess",
   "t": "Да, он за своим столом.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Что он хочет?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "hostess",
   "t": "Я не знаю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Хорошо, я иду.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "Here are the lines up close.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "hostess",
   "t": "Здравствуйте.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Hello.' This is the long polite hello, zdravstvuyte, and in speech the first v is silent: zdrastvuyte.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Доброе утро.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Good morning.' Russian says dobroe utro. The word utro, 'morning', is neuter, so dobroe, 'good', ends in -oe.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "hostess",
   "t": "Дима тебя ждёт.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Dima's waiting for you.' The name Dima is the friendly short form of Dmitry, and here it means the owner. The word tebya, 'you', comes before zhdyot, 'is waiting'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Дима? Он здесь?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Dima? He's here?' Russian drops 'is' here: on zdes is literally 'he here'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "hostess",
   "t": "Да, он за своим столом.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Yes, he's at his table.' Russian says za stolom, literally 'behind the table', for sitting at one.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Что он хочет?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'What does he want?' The word khochet, 'he wants', comes from khotet, 'to want', which shifts its sounds: khochu is 'I want'.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "hostess",
   "t": "Я не знаю.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'I don't know.' Russian puts ne, 'not', straight in front of the action: ya ne znayu.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Хорошо, я иду.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Okay, I'm coming.' Russian says ya idu, literally 'I am going', where English says 'I'm coming'. And the whole exchange again.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "hostess",
   "t": "Здравствуйте.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Доброе утро.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "hostess",
   "t": "Дима тебя ждёт.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Дима? Он здесь?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "hostess",
   "t": "Да, он за своим столом.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Что он хочет?",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "hostess",
   "t": "Я не знаю.",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "barinov",
   "t": "Хорошо, я иду.",
   "gap": 0.9
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "So Barinov goes to face the owner. Most Russian names have a friendly short form, and Dima is the one for Dmitry. See you next time!",
   "gap": 0.5
  },
  {
   "v": "hostess",
   "t": "Удачи!",
   "gap": 0.3
  }
 ],
